WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:05.031
Joe examined the leg iron and declared that it had been filed open a long time

2
00:00:05.032 --> 00:00:11.047
ago. I believed it to be my convict's leg iron. But I did not believe that my

3
00:00:11.048 --> 00:00:17.951
convict would hurt my sister. I'm not sure why, but I suspected her attacker was either

4
00:00:17.952 --> 00:00:21.602
Orlick or the man in the pub who'd stirred his drink with the file. It was

5
00:00:21.603 --> 00:00:25.253
horrible to think that I had provided the weapon even indirectly. I suffered terribly while I

6
00:00:25.254 --> 00:00:28.618
considered whether to tell Joe. But I didn't say anything for fear of making him angry with

7
00:00:28.619 --> 00:00:35.269
me. The local constables and some London policemen came to the house several times for a

8
00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:43.507
week or two, but they never discovered the culprit. My sister lay very ill in bed.

9
00:00:43.508 --> 00:00:50.499
Her sight was disturbed; her hearing and memory were greatly impaired; and her speech was unintelligible.

10
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.586
When at last she was able to come downstairs, we kept my slate by her side so

11
00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:57.515
that she could write what she could not speak. Her temper was greatly improved, however, and

12
00:00:57.516 --> 00:01:01.245
she seemed more patient. We were at a loss to find someone to take care of

13
00:01:01.246 --> 00:01:07.832
her. Then Biddy's grandmother died, and Biddy came to live with us. She became a blessing

14
00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:12.071
to the household, especially to Joe, for the dear fellow was brokenhearted about my sister's condition. Biddy's

15
00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:18.888
first triumph was to solve a puzzle that had completely defeated me. Again and again my

16
00:01:18.889 --> 00:01:23.308
sister had drawn something on the slate that looked like a T . She seemed eager

17
00:01:23.309 --> 00:01:30.538
for us to bring it to her. I tried everything from tar to toast to tub,

18
00:01:30.539 --> 00:01:35.221
but she wanted none of those. Biddy looked at the T and immediately recognized a hammer. "Don't

19
00:01:35.222 --> 00:01:38.800
you see?" she said. "It's Orlick!" My sister had forgotten his name and could only identify

20
00:01:38.801 --> 00:01:44.038
him by his hammer. Joe and I rushed to the forge and told Orlick to go

21
00:01:44.039 --> 00:01:46.784
to the kitchen. He slowly laid down his hammer, wiped his brow with his apron, and

22
00:01:46.785 --> 00:01:51.292
went slouching into the house. I hoped my sister was going to denounce him. Instead she seemed

23
00:01:51.293 --> 00:01:54.189
pleased when he arrived and motioned that she would give him a drink. After that she

24
00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:58.205
asked for him to come every day. Like the rest of us, Orlick seemed to have

25
00:01:58.206 --> 00:02:04.619
no idea what to make of this. I now fell into a regular routine of apprenticeship

26
00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:10.268
life, which only varied when I visited Miss Havisham on my birthday. Each time I found her

27
00:02:10.269 --> 00:02:14.260
just as I'd left her, and each time she spoke of Estella in the very same

28
00:02:14.261 --> 00:02:23.427
way. Miss Havisham always gave me a pound and told me to come back next year.

29
00:02:23.428 --> 00:02:28.252
So unchanging was the dull old house, the yellow light in the darkened room, and the

30
00:02:28.253 --> 00:02:31.744
faded ghost sitting in the chair that I felt as if time had stopped there. Slowly I

31
00:02:31.745 --> 00:02:37.593
became conscious of a change in Biddy, who had always been so untidy. Now her hair

32
00:02:37.594 --> 00:02:41.744
was neat and her hands were always clean. She was not beautiful—she was common and could

33
00:02:41.745 --> 00:02:46.974
not compare to Estella—but she was very pleasant. One night about a year after she came

34
00:02:46.975 --> 00:02:50.395
to us, we were sitting by the fire. She was sewing, and I was copying some passages

35
00:02:50.396 --> 00:02:53.649
from a book in an effort to improve myself. I looked up and suddenly noticed what

36
00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:58.569
pretty eyes she had. "Biddy, how do you manage to learn everything that I learn?" I

37
00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:01.982
was beginning to be rather vain about my knowledge and always spent my birthday money on books.

38
00:03:01.983 --> 00:03:08.053
"I might as well ask how you manage," she said. "When I come in from the

39
00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:16.045
forge at night, anyone can see me working at learning. But you never work at it."

40
00:03:16.046 --> 00:03:19.704
"I suppose I must catch it like a cough," said Biddy quietly and went on with

41
00:03:19.705 --> 00:03:25.204
her sewing. "You know, I was your first teacher." I felt bad then that I hadn't expressed

42
00:03:25.205 --> 00:03:30.379
more gratitude for this. "We must talk together a little more, as we used to," I

43
00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:33.371
said, and I proposed a walk on the marshes. And so the next Sunday, we walked

44
00:03:33.372 --> 00:03:37.871
beyond the village and the church until we were out on the marshes. As usual I

45
00:03:37.872 --> 00:03:43.696
thought of Estella and Miss Havisham. I decided that now was a good time and place to

46
00:03:43.697 --> 00:03:47.847
take Biddy into my confidence. "Biddy," I said seriously, "I don't want to be a common

47
00:03:47.848 --> 00:03:56.442
person. I want to be a gentleman." "You know best, Pip, but don't you think you're

48
00:03:56.443 --> 00:04:04.522
happier as you are?" "I'm not at all happy as I am!" I exclaimed impatiently. "I'm

49
00:04:04.523 --> 00:04:08.839
disgusted with my work and my life. Don't be absurd." "Was I absurd?" Biddy quietly raised her

50
00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:15.172
eyebrows. "I'm sorry. I only want you to do well and be comfortable." "If only I

51
00:04:15.173 --> 00:04:20.918
could have settled down and been as fond of the forge as I used to be.

52
00:04:20.919 --> 00:04:28.149
Perhaps Joe and I could have become partners and I might have courted you. I'd have

53
00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:34.728
been good enough for you, wouldn't I?" "Yes, but I'm not very particular." That didn't sound flattering,

54
00:04:34.729 --> 00:04:40.070
but I knew she meant well. "Instead," I went on, "I'm dissatisfied and unhappy. If only

55
00:04:40.071 --> 00:04:43.720
someone hadn't told me I was coarse and common . . ." Biddy turned toward me.

56
00:04:43.721 --> 00:04:46.490
"That was neither a very true nor a very polite thing to say. Who said it?"

57
00:04:46.491 --> 00:04:50.403
"Estella. I admire her very much and I want to be a gentleman on her account." "Do

58
00:04:50.404 --> 00:04:56.062
you want to be a gentleman to spite her or to win her over?" "I don't

59
00:04:56.063 --> 00:05:00.292
know!" I cried desperately. Biddy patted my hand and told me she was glad I'd confided

60
00:05:00.293 --> 00:05:05.133
in her. I gave her a kiss and said, "I'll always tell you everything." "Until you're

61
00:05:05.134 --> 00:05:08.554
a gentleman." We walked on, talking as we went, and everything she said seemed right. Biddy was

62
00:05:08.555 --> 00:05:11.633
never insulting. She never derived pleasure from giving me pain. "Oh, Biddy, I wish I could

63
00:05:11.634 --> 00:05:17.204
get myself to fall in love with you." "But you never will, you see," she said

64
00:05:17.205 --> 00:05:22.950
firmly. She was so certain on that point that it hurt my feelings. But in my

65
00:05:22.951 --> 00:05:28.109
heart I knew she was right. After that we turned for home. When we reached the churchyard,

66
00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:32.435
Orlick suddenly appeared. He asked where we were going, and when I told him we were

67
00:05:32.436 --> 00:05:36.339
going home, he said he'd accompany us. "Don't let him come," whispered Biddy. "I don't like

68
00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:39.006
him." Since I didn't like him either, I told him we could manage by ourselves. He

69
00:05:39.007 --> 00:05:40.840
dropped back but continued to follow us. "Why don't you like him, Biddy?" She glanced over her

70
00:05:40.841 --> 00:05:45.093
shoulder. "Because I'm afraid he likes me." I kept an eye on Orlick after that night.

71
00:05:45.094 --> 00:05:52.418
If he hadn't become such a favorite of my sister, I would have tried to get

72
00:05:52.419 --> 00:05:58.331
him dismissed. Some days I was certain that Biddy was immeasurably better than Estella and that

73
00:05:58.332 --> 00:06:01.428
the simple working life was best for me. But then I would think of Estella and feel

74
00:06:01.429 --> 00:06:06.476
confused again. This confusion might have gone on forever, had not the most astonishing thing happened.

